2	// XERJ — share-link guest page
3	//
4	// The whole guest side of `xerj share`: read the share id from the URL
5	// fragment, exchange it plus a passcode for a read-only key, then give the
6	// guest a reading room over the one index that key can reach.
7	//
8	// THREE RULES THIS FILE KEEPS — the tests grep for the first and drive a real
9	// browser at the other two:
10	//
11	//   1. Document text is attacker-controlled. It is other people's email. It is
12	//      only ever put on the page as TEXT — `textContent` / `createTextNode` —
13	//      and never parsed as markup. There is no `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`,
14	//      `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write`, `eval` or `new Function` in
15	//      this file, and an HTML email body is shown as the text it is. URLs in
16	//      documents are not turned into links.
17	//   2. The share id is never part of a URL this page requests. It lives in
18	//      the URL *fragment*, which a browser does not send to a server, and it
19	//      goes to the node in the BODY of the claim POST (`/_share/claim`) —
20	//      never in a path. A path is what an access log, a reverse proxy and a
21	//      tunnel's edge write down; the first cut posted to `/_share/<id>/claim`
22	//      and undid the fragment one request later. After a claim the id is
23	//      removed from the address bar.
24	//   3. The session is ONE sessionStorage record, written exactly as
25	//        sessionStorage['xerj.share'] =
26	//          JSON.stringify({api_key, index, brain, expires_at, label})
27	//      — the console's guest mode reads the same record. sessionStorage, not
